-- Generated by `pnpm decisions:seed` from docs/planning/decisions.md (decision 191): the log's
-- entries as decisions, numbered as in the log, active unless a later entry replaces them.
-- Do not edit by hand.
insert into public.decisions
  (number, statement, reason, status, decided_by_name, decided_at, origin, origin_ref)
overriding system value
values
  (201, 'For slice 2.6, before areas have owners: an active decision is never edited in place; to change one, a person asks the teammate in a thread to record a decision that supersedes it. Whoever may confirm a proposal (G11: someone who wrote in the thread it came from) may also reject it or fix its wording before confirming. Slack''s Confirm follows the same rule as the web app, changing 192''s "anyone in the workspace"', 'Chosen in conversation, all as recommended. The record never changes under people who relied on it, and one rule in both places is one rule to explain', 'active', 'the owner', '2026-11-25T00:00:00Z', 'import', '201'),
  (202, 'How slice 2.6 is built (`slice-2-6.md`). The API decides who may act on a proposal: for one the teammate recorded, a person whose message the bot received in that thread (someone else joins by mentioning the bot there); for one from listening, a person in its `confirmable_by`; always in the decision''s source and workspace; an imported decision is read-only. Confirm, reject and edit (statement, reason, area) are routes a signed-in person may call, the first person writes, and the person comes from the token, never the body; Slack''s Confirm calls the same rule and tells a person refused why, privately. Every change after a decision is recorded goes into `decision_events` with who, when, and the text before and after. `GET /v1/decisions/:number` returns a decision with its history and what the caller may do. The web app adds `/decisions` (by area, search, status) and `/decisions/<number>`, whose forms post to the web app''s own server, refused unless from its own pages, which calls the API as the person. A thread proposal''s confirmation or rejection is noted in its thread; one from listening is not (176)', 'A teammate thread keeps only the messages sent to the bot, so "wrote in the thread" can be checked only for those; mentioning the bot is how anyone else takes part. The person from the token keeps a page from acting for someone else. Keeping the before and after of each edit makes the wiki correctable without losing what was there', 'active', 'the owner', '2026-11-25T00:00:00Z', 'import', '202')
on conflict (origin, origin_ref) where origin_ref is not null do nothing;
